{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    /*
     * THE RESOLUTION CHANGE, AND IT IS THE ONLY REASON THIS FILE LEFT THE
     * FROZEN SET THE INTERFACE WORK WAS HELD TO.
     *
     * This repo was `module: NodeNext` with no `paths`, so every relative
     * import carries an explicit `.js`. shadcn's output uses `@/` aliases and
     * extensionless imports and compiles under neither. The swap was MEASURED
     * rather than asserted: `ESNext` + `Bundler` typechecked clean,
     * the suite stayed green, the build stayed green, and **every existing
     * `.js`-suffixed import kept working** — which was the thing assumed to
     * break. Bundler resolution accepts both spellings, which is the point.
     *
     * Nothing else here moves. `rootDir`, `strict`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`
     * and `noUnusedLocals` are all as they were.
     */
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM"],
    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": false,
    "noEmit": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "types": ["vitest/globals", "vite/client"],
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "rootDir": "src"
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "src/**/*.tsx"],
  /*
   * TWO TESTS ARE TYPECHECKED BY THE APPLICATION'S CONFIG INSTEAD OF BY THIS
   * ONE, AND THEY ARE THE TWO THAT REACH ACROSS.
   *
   * `tsconfig.build.json` records the measurement this split rests on: no file
   * outside the application imports the application - only TEST files do, and
   * tests are excluded from the build. Those tests still exist and they still
   * reach. Under THIS config only half of what they import can be resolved,
   * because the application's `@/` alias and its view of this package's
   * subpaths belong to the application's config.
   *
   * So they are read by `apps/wallet/tsconfig.json`, which can see both halves,
   * and they are excluded here. THEY ARE STILL RUN BY THIS PACKAGE'S OWN
   * `vitest` - a runner reads its include glob and not this file - so nothing
   * this package tests stops being tested. What moves is which compiler reads
   * them, and the alternative was to leave a config claiming to typecheck a
   * file it cannot resolve.
   */
  "exclude": [
    "src/profile/registry-open.test.tsx",
    "src/profile/unlock.test.ts"
  ]
}
